  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Losartan, a member of the angiotensin II receptor antagonists, is linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its relationship with Valsartan, a closely related compound that is known to treat this condition. The evidence suggests that Losartan, through its structural similarities to other angiotensin II receptor antagonists like Tasosartan and Forasartan, shares a common pathway with Valsartan in modulating physiological processes that are associated with diabetes. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds—such as the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonist and Biphenylyltetrazole—highlight a potential mechanism by which these drugs may influence metabolic pathways, possibly through receptor activation or signaling that affects insulin sensitivity or glucose metabolism. The recurring involvement of angiotensin II receptor antagonists in the treatment of diabetes underscores a broader pharmacological relationship, where structural similarity and shared biological targets may contribute to therapeutic effects.",
